 A method to approximate radiance distribution on mesh triangles where emission direction varies across the surface, using clamped cosine lobes distributed over an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) of the intersection area projection. It handles non-homogeneous emission by interpolating emission directions from triangle vertices and computing a spherical harmonic that approximates the radiance distribution for each intersected LPV cell. Choose this when the area light source has varying emission directions across its surface.
 
-### Textured Area Lights Sampling
-A method to store and sample radiant flux information on area light surfaces using texture mapping and mipmapping to approximate the entire radiant flux of an intersection area [S1, unofficial]. It uses texture coordinates at triangle vertices, interpolates them to find the texture coordinate at the AABB center of the intersection area, and applies mipmapping to quickly compute the radiant flux [S1, unofficial]. Choose this when the area light source has varying radiant flux across its surface, represented by a texture map.
+### [Textured Area Lights Sampling](15821-textured-area-lights-sampling.md)
+A method to store and sample radiant flux information on area light surfaces using texture mapping and mipmapping to approximate the entire radiant flux of an intersection area. It uses texture coordinates at triangle vertices, interpolates them to find the texture coordinate at the AABB center of the intersection area, and applies mipmapping to quickly compute the radiant flux. Choose this when the area light source has varying radiant flux across its surface, represented by a texture map.
+
+### [AABB-based Emission Direction Sampling](20669-aabb-based-emission-direction-sampling.md)
+A method to approximate the radiance distribution of a triangle mesh when the emission direction varies across its surface, by using an emission direction per vertex and interpolating within an Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) of the projected intersection area. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by avoiding complex spherical harmonic fitting for non-homogeneous lights or texture-based flux maps, instead using a set of clamped cosine lobes distributed between interpolated corner directions of the projected AABB. Choose this when directional variation must be captured without texture lookups or heavy SH computation.
 
